# NO NAMESERVER LINE, DELIBERATELY.  This file is staged by about thirty
# harnesses and its only interface is a2065.device on DHCP, so the lease is
# what carries the name servers on every rig this tree runs on.
#
# It said `nameserver 10.0.2.3` until 2026-08-27 -- fs-uae's SLIRP forwarder,
# which exists on no real segment.  The resolver asks it first because a file
# server outranks a leased one, waits about a second, and re-sends the query
# to the server the lease supplied.  On the wire that is two packets and one
# second per name, on every harness that looks anything up, and the lookups
# succeeded either way, which is why it survived a rewrite of the comment
# above it: the line before this one already recorded the same shape of
# defect, 10.0.2.2 for 10.0.2.3, and fixed the address rather than the idea
# that this file should name a server at all.
#
#   nameserver 10.0.2.2    gethostbyname("example.com")   30.06 s
#   nameserver 10.0.2.3    gethostbyname("example.com")    0.58 s   (SLIRP)
#   nameserver 10.0.2.3    first lookup, bridged          +1 s, 2 packets
#   no nameserver line     first lookup, bridged           the lease's own
#
# A harness that needs a server this file cannot know -- a static address, no
# DHCP, a captive resolver -- writes its own over this one at stage time and
# reads it off the emulator host: tests/tools/run-nettools.sh and
# tests/tools/run-dnssearch.sh both do, with
#
#   awk '/^nameserver/ && $2 !~ /:/ { print $2; exit }' /etc/resolv.conf
#
# and both refuse a 127.x answer, because a bridged guest cannot reach the
# emulator host's loopback.  Copy that, do not put an address here.
#
# The thirty seconds above is the resolver's own failover time and is worth a
# look on its own account; see docs/RESEARCH.md.
domain localdomain
